---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

// The short version. The unabridged case studies still render, unchanged,
// at /csi/full via CsiShowcase — this page is the front door, not a
// replacement for the record.
const [profile] = await getCollection('profile');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Room or Radio? — Braeden Keena"
  description="A Wi-Fi device classifier, the physical controls that broke its interpretation, and what the signal turned out to measure instead."
>
  <Nav name={profile.data.name} />

  <article class="csi-page">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide">
      <header class="csi-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">Technical Investigation · Wireless Sensing</p>
        <h1 class="csi-title">Room or Radio?</h1>
        <p class="csi-subtitle">
          I built a classifier that identified three Wi-Fi transmitters with 99.7% accuracy, then
          spent two months proving it wasn't measuring what I said it was.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- Numbers at a glance -->
      <div class="glance">
        <div class="glance-item">
          <span class="glance-num">69,510,186</span>
          <span class="glance-lbl">clean beacon frames replayed, screened from 69,688,145 raw rows — the 54.92 GiB canonical subset of a 99.5 GiB corpus</span>
        </div>
        <div class="glance-item">
          <span class="glance-num">0.2σ / 19.6σ</span>
          <span class="glance-lbl">separation when three boards share one surface, versus one board moved between rooms</span>
        </div>
        <div class="glance-item">
          <span class="glance-num">29 / 35</span>
          <span class="glance-lbl">hypotheses killed across formal rejection tests — 13 of them my own working results</span>
        </div>
        <div class="glance-item">
          <span class="glance-num">8 nights</span>
          <span class="glance-lbl">paired overnight captures, three transmitters, two receiver architectures</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- 1 -->
      <section class="csi-section">
        <h2>1. The number I believed</h2>
        <p>
          The first classifier identified three ESP32 transmitters at <strong>99.7% accuracy over
          7,497 windows</strong> on a chronological holdout. That figure carries a condition and
          should never travel without it: <strong>desk receiver alone.</strong> Once a second
          receiver's captures of the same sessions join the population, it falls to
          <strong>95.7% over 14,234 windows</strong>. The gap is cross-receiver generalization,
          not a harder set of devices.
        </p>
        <p>
          The feature was subcarrier phase slope, which I attributed to quartz crystal sampling
          frequency offset. Phase slope is actually a composite — sampling frequency offset,
          packet-boundary timing offset, linear channel delay, and estimator error — and a single
          antenna cannot decouple them. The accuracy was real. The physical attribution was wrong.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 2 -->
      <section class="csi-section">
        <h2>2. The controls that broke it</h2>
        <p>
          In the original setup each beacon sat in a different room. <em>Which radio is this?</em>
          and <em>where is this radio?</em> were the same label, and nothing in the experiment could
          tell them apart.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three tests separated them. Placing all three transmitters on one surface four feet apart
          collapsed between-device separation from 7.9σ to <strong>0.2σ</strong>. Moving a single
          board to another room produced a <strong>19.6σ</strong> shift against a 2.5–4.9σ
          background drift floor. And two receivers watching the same transmitter in the same
          instant disagreed by <strong>6.79</strong> standard deviations in between-unit SD —
          the median across 10-second, 1-minute and 5-minute bins — with near-zero correlation
          between them, often reporting opposite slope signs.
        </p>
        <p>
          A property of a quartz crystal cannot change because a board crossed a doorway, and it
          cannot disagree between two listeners. The classifier was reading room geometry.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 3 -->
      <section class="csi-section">
        <h2>3. Eight nights</h2>
        <p>
          The replication ran three transmitters four feet apart on one surface, two distinct
          receiver architectures (ESP32-D0WD and ESP32-S3), across <strong>eight paired overnight
          captures</strong> — sixteen receiver-nights in total. All 69,688,145 raw rows were
          screened and 69,510,186 clean frames replayed through a deterministic, unwrap-free
          estimator at 1-second, 10-second, 1-minute and 5-minute aggregations.
        </p>
        <p>
          Train and test split across whole nights, never across frames. Nearby CSI frames
          correlate strongly, so a random frame split rewards temporal interpolation and reports it
          as recognition.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4 -->
      <section class="csi-section">
        <h2>4. What it actually measures</h2>
        <p>
          Within a single session, a fixed link stays recognizable: <strong>88.4% on D0WD, 91.7% on
          S3, 94.3% fused.</strong> That result is <strong>exploratory</strong> — it was specified
          after the frozen tests, using a train-on-first-40%, discard-the-middle-20%,
          test-on-last-40% split, and it is labelled that way in the exit report rather than
          presented alongside the preregistered results.
        </p>
        <p>
          A blind 30-minute held-out window across fourteen receiver-nights reached
          <strong>95.8%</strong> against a 33.3% chance baseline, with signatures shifting only
          0.07σ across a 39-minute gap.
        </p>
        <p>
          Across unseen nights the preregistered generalization test failed. Best channel-feature
          accuracy was 53.2% on D0WD and 64.3% on S3 — and "best" is doing work there, because a
          template carried to a <em>different</em> night averages <strong>38.6–48.8%</strong>, with
          individual pairs ranging from near 0% to near 100%. Claimed-link verification reached an
          equal-error rate of 30.9% against a preregistered target of 15%, AUC 0.700 against a
          target of 0.90. Concatenating two receivers did not rescue it.
        </p>
        <p class="pull">
          This is not a persistent device fingerprint. It is a high-resolution, short-lived
          measurement of a physical link.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 5 -->
      <section class="csi-section">
        <h2>5. The more interesting security question</h2>
        <p>
          A MAC address is spoofable in software in milliseconds. A physical propagation path is
          not. To imitate a link signature an attacker would have to reproduce the multipath seen
          by several distributed receivers, from an authorized location, orientation and
          environment, at the same time.
        </p>
        <p>
          The eight nights do not show that spoofing is impossible — no cloned MACs, directional
          spoofers, channel emulators or relay attacks were ever run. What they show is that the
          link is concrete enough to be worth challenging. Any protocol built on it would need
          short-horizon enrollment, multi-receiver consensus, and randomized challenge-response,
          because passive CSI alone drifts over days.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 6 -->
      <section class="csi-section">
        <h2>6. What the project demonstrates</h2>
        <p>
          The transferable part isn't fitting lines to phase data. It's building an embedded
          instrument, discovering that a 99.7% metric answered the wrong question, and narrowing the
          claim until it matched what the hardware could actually support.
        </p>
        <p>
          Thirty-five formal rejection tests killed twenty-nine distinct hypotheses. Fifteen of the
          thirty-five were scored against thresholds frozen before the test ran.
          <strong>Thirteen of the twenty-nine were my own working results</strong>, retired by
          controls I built specifically to permit that outcome. Results that went against me are
          published beside the ones that didn't.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 7 -->
      <section class="csi-section">
        <h2>7. What remains unisolated</h2>
        <ul class="limits">
          <li>
            <strong>Adversarial resistance is unmeasured.</strong> Repeatability and separability
            were tested. Cloned MACs, directional spoofers, channel emulators and relay attacks
            were not.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Space and hardware stay partly confounded.</strong> Four feet at 2.4 GHz is
            roughly ten wavelengths, and each beacon kept its own antenna and position.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Packet-boundary timing offsets.</strong> Every capture carries an unknown
            detection offset that mimics a phase slope, and a single-antenna receiver cannot
            separate it from crystal drift.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Receiver asymmetry.</strong> Gain control and analog front-end differences
            between the D0WD and S3 silicon were never isolated.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The unit of analysis is three radios.</strong> 69.5 million frames buy
            statistical power over time, not over devices. Every multi-class number here is a
            three-class problem against a 33.3% baseline.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Full record -->
      <section class="full-record">
        <h3>The full record</h3>
        <p>
          This page is the short version. The unabridged case studies — the V1 instrument build,
          every rejection test with its threshold, and the complete investigation narrative — are
          published in full.
        </p>
        <a href="/csi/full" class="btn-full">Read the full record ↗</a>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>

  <Footer profile={profile} />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .csi-page {
    padding-block: clamp(48px, 8vw, 96px);
  }

  .csi-header {
    max-width: 860px;
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .csi-title {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: clamp(2.3rem, 5vw, 3.8rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .csi-subtitle {
    margin-top: 18px;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .glance {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--border-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    border-radius: 10px;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-bottom: clamp(40px, 6vw, 72px);
  }

  .glance-item {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 8px;
    padding: 20px 22px;
    background: var(--bg);
  }

  .glance-num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .glance-lbl {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .csi-section {
    max-width: 780px;
    margin-bottom: clamp(36px, 5vw, 56px);
  }

  .csi-section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 2.4vw, 1.7rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .csi-section p {
    margin-bottom: 14px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .csi-section strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .pull {
    margin-top: 20px;
    padding-left: 18px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text-primary) !important;
  }

  .limits {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  .limits li {
    padding: 14px 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    border-radius: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .full-record {
    max-width: 780px;
    margin-top: clamp(48px, 7vw, 80px);
    padding: 28px 30px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .full-record h3 {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .full-record p {
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .btn-full {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 12px 22px;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .btn-full:hover {
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
